// disk_controller — one of the slice's --entries threads, and the pipeline's
// coordinator.
//
// AXVERITY_EXTENT_WRITE_PATH step 3 turned it from a pure passthrough into a
// QUEUE. It used to drive an exact loop_count forwarding each sealed block's
// bundle one hop to flusher — a controller with nothing to control. It now
// drains ONE channel fed by TWO producers (mem_controller's write bundles and
// the tick thread's SLA ticks), coalesces redundant instructions, and acts.
//
// The bound moved twice. loop_count(n) counted MESSAGES, which equalled blocks
// only while every message was a block. Step 3 moved it to "n writes
// forwarded", which is right about ticks but still rests on a PREDICTED n —
// and step 5a showed a tick can seal a partial block no prediction ever saw.
// It bounds on the end-of-stream sentinel now, which is posted after the last
// seal actually happens and so cannot drift from the run.
//
// Still no raw-memory touch and no disk I/O here.
fn disk_controller_main(argv: TextList) -> Unit {
  let _done = loop_while(Text("0"), wp_dc_more, wp_dc_step, Int(1000000000))
  Unit()
}
